<!-- GridWindow: the merged "Continue" screen (lib/ui/gridwindow.py).

     ONE ROW PER BAND. Each band (resume / next up / watched / library)
     has its own labelled header and its own horizontally-scrolling poster
     row, stacked in a vertical `grouplist` (30001). The focused band is
     pinned at the bottom of the 1920x1080 skin. Up and down switch
     bands, and only the focused band is on screen. This is Kodi's own
     idiom for sectioned content:
     skin.estuary's Home builds its widget rows exactly this way
     (`WidgetListPoster` inside `WidgetGroupListCommon`, each preceded by
     a `CategoryLabel`).

     Two earlier attempts are recorded so they are not retried:
       - Header rows interleaved into ONE wrapping `panel`. A panel gives
         every row the item layout's full height, so each header cost a
         whole poster row of screen.
       - One band at a time behind a tab strip. It hid three quarters of
         the screen to label four groups.

     THE FOCUSED BAND IS PINNED. Kodi's grouplist top-aligns the
     focused control on the way up and bottom-aligns it on the way down.
     Those land on the same scroll position only when that control is
     exactly as tall as the viewport. Each band is a group of height
     396, the grouplist is 396 tall starting at y=684, and `itemgap` is
     0. A negative gap would pull the next header into the band; zero
     leaves exactly one band on screen. The artwork stays 200x300 (a
     true 2:3, the same poster Home uses) so the title, badge, and
     focus frame fit in that one row. The row is still wider than the
     frame, so the last poster bleeds off the right edge.

     The group is what scrolls, not the panel. AddControl rewires
     onup/ondown on direct children only, so each panel sets that chain
     itself. A hidden band's panel still points at the next one: Kodi
     follows the chain and stops if it loops, which is what skips an
     empty row without spinning.

     Focus is a bright frame around the poster plus the accent underline.
     The unfocused poster is dimmed, so the frame is the thing a remote
     press has to show: a 4px line on its own was easy to miss from the
     sofa, and the click sound still played. A zoom was tried and removed.
     Kodi applies a layout animation to the whole cell GROUP - poster and
     labels together - so a 6% zoom grew a focused card into the
     neighbouring poster and up into the hero caption. The frame does not
     move the cell, so it cannot do that.


     `usecontrolcoords` is what makes the stack work: each control is laid
     out under the previous one, so an empty row collapses and the bands
     below it move up. A viewer with nothing finished sees no "Recently
     watched" heading, and up/down navigation never lands on a row that
     is not there.

     Both are gated on a WINDOW PROPERTY (`band_<name>`) that
     `GridWindow.onInit()` sets before it focuses anything - deliberately
     NOT on `Integer.IsGreater(Container(id).NumItems,0)`, which is the
     obvious condition and is wrong twice over.

     `Container(id).NumItems` does not update synchronously with
     `addItems()`: measured on a real device it still reads 0 in the same
     pass and settles ~100ms later. A panel carrying that condition is
     still INVISIBLE when `onInit()` calls `setFocusId()` on it, Kodi
     refuses to focus an invisible control ("Control 30002 in window
     13003 has been asked to focus, but it can't"), and a window with
     nothing focused never redraws - it looks frozen while every row is
     correctly populated underneath. Dropping the gate entirely fixed the
     freeze but left empty rows visible, and up/down navigation then
     bounced off them. A window property set from Python is true the
     moment the row is filled, so it satisfies both.

     THE CELL LAYOUTS ARE GENERATED: all four rows carry a byte-identical
     copy, because this skin has no Includes.xml to share one through.
     Keep them in sync - tests/test_gridwindow.py asserts they are.

     The poster box is a true 2:3, so `aspectratio=keep` draws a standard
     poster edge to edge inside it. That is what lets the 200px progress
     track and the labels line up with the ARTWORK rather than with a
     wider box - at an earlier 240 box the rendered poster was only 216
     wide and the bar overhung it by 12px a side.
     `gridwindow.PROGRESS_TRACK_WIDTH` must equal that 200.

     Band identity is carried by colour as well as by the headers - the
     badge is tinted per band (gridwindow._BAND_COLOURS), the header takes
     the same tint, and finished titles are drawn dimmed - so a card still
     says which band it belongs to once its header has scrolled off. -->
<window>
    <depth>0.52</depth>
    <coordinates><left>0</left><top>0</top></coordinates>
    <controls>
        <control type="group">
            <left>0</left><top>0</top>
            <visible>!Player.HasVideo</visible>
            <animation type="WindowOpen" reversible="false">
                <effect type="fade" delay="0" end="100" time="200" />
            </animation>
            <!-- No WindowClose animation on purpose: Kodi drops every action while the topmost modal plays one (test_skin_xml.py rule 9). -->

            <!-- Full-screen fanart. The left fade keeps the title readable.
                 The row band below is a solid plate, so the art stops there. -->
            <control type="image"><width>1920</width><height>1080</height><texture colordiffuse="FF0A0C0E">white.png</texture></control>
            <control type="image" id="30000"><width>1920</width><height>1080</height><texture background="true" colordiffuse="FFFFFFFF"></texture><aspectratio>scale</aspectratio></control>
            <control type="image"><width>1280</width><height>1080</height><texture colordiffuse="E60A0C0E">fade_h.png</texture></control>
            <!-- fade_v.png is opaque only from about 75% of its height.
                 312 + 0.75*496 = 684, the top of the one visible row.
                 The solid plate under it keeps the art off that row. -->
            <control type="image"><left>0</left><top>312</top><width>1920</width><height>496</height><texture colordiffuse="FF0A0C0E">fade_v.png</texture></control>
            <control type="image"><left>0</left><top>684</top><width>1920</width><height>396</height><texture colordiffuse="FF0A0C0E">white.png</texture></control>

            <!-- Set at runtime by GridWindow: "RevRivulet / CONTINUE". -->
            <control type="image"><left>96</left><top>60</top><width>34</width><height>34</height><texture>logo.png</texture><aspectratio>keep</aspectratio></control>
            <control type="label" id="30006"><left>150</left><top>60</top><width>900</width><height>34</height><font>font10</font><textcolor>4DEEF3F6</textcolor>
                <label></label>
            </control>

            <!-- Hero, upper left, over the full-screen fanart. Title max
                 height is 64. The caption sits under it. The description
                 is two lines and scrolls. It ends at y=348, above the row
                 band, so the overview cannot run into a header. -->
            <control type="label" id="30020"><left>96</left><top>172</top><width>1400</width><height>64</height><font>font37</font><textcolor>FFFFFFFF</textcolor><aligny>center</aligny>
